\n\nLine y = x passes through (2,2),(3,3),(4,4) not present, (5,5) not present. So covers (2,2),(3,3). That's 2 points.\n\nLine y = x + 1 passes through (2,3),(3,4) not present, (4,5) not present, (5,6) not present. So covers (2,3) only.\n\nLine y = x + 2 passes through (2,4),(3,5) not present, etc. So covers (2,4) only.\n\nLine y = x - 2 passes through (3,1),(4,2),(5,3) not present. So covers (3,1),(4,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine y = x - 3 passes through (4,1),(5,2) not present. So covers (4,1) only.\n\nLine y = x - 4 passes through (5,1) only.\n\nThus using slope 1 lines we can cover many points: (2,1)-(3,2), (2,2)-(3,3), (2,3) alone, (2,4) alone, (3,1)-(4,2), (4,1) alone, (5,1) alone. That's 7 lines needed.\n\nBut we can also use other slopes.\n\nLet's try slope 2 lines: y = 2x + c. For integer points, we need to find c such that points are integer. For (2,1): 1 = 4 + c => c = -3. So line L: y = 2x - 3 passes through (2,1). Does it pass through any other points? For x=3, y = 6-3 = 3 => (3,3) is present. For x=4, y = 8-3 = 5 not present. For x=5, y = 10-3 = 7 not present. So L covers (2,1),(3,3). That's 2 points.\n\nLine through (2,2): 2 = 4 + c => c = -2 => y = 2x - 2. For x=3, y = 6-2 = 4 not present. For x=4, y = 8-2 = 6 not present. So only (2,2).\n\nLine through (2,3): 3 = 4 + c => c = -1 => y = 2x - 1. For x=3, y = 6-1 = 5 not present. So only (2,3).\n\nLine through (2,4): 4 = 4 + c => c = 0 => y = 2x. For x=3, y = 6 not present. So only (2,4).\n\nLine through (3,1): 1 = 6 + c => c = -5 => y = 2x -5. For x=4, y = 8-5 = 3 not present. For x=5, y = 10-5 = 5 not present. So only (3,1).\n\nLine through (3,2): 2 = 6 + c => c = -4 => y = 2x -4. For x=4, y = 8-4 = 4 not present. So only (3,2).\n\nLine through (4,1): 1 = 8 + c => c = -7 => y = 2x -7. For x=5, y = 10-7 = 3 not present. So only (4,1).\n\nLine through (5,1): 1 = 10 + c => c = -9 => y = 2x -9. For x=4, y = 8-9 = -1 not present. So only (5,1).\n\nThus slope 2 lines can at most cover 2 points (like (2,1)-